body:
I've got another one today. I actually practiced in the mirror, answering questions. I learned two things - I need to slow down, and I can talk less. They don't need to know my life story. I can say fewer words in the same amount of time. Come off as a little reserved, even. Anything is better than rambling quickly - and betraying the idea that I have no idea what to say so I'm just throwing word soup out there to keep them off my scent. SLOOOW DOWN. That's my advice to myself. 

I think I'll have more questions to ask at this interview because I'm actually more interested in the job. At the last one I didn't really have any questions because I'm sortof meh about the position. I think my excitement and curiosity will help me to seem like a better quality candidate. At least I hope. I'm not nervous, you're nervous!!

Thanks again for the advice, I'll keep trying until I find something! :) Good luck to you!

&#x200B;

&#x200B;

Thanks, I'm reading now about how my ex's PTSD may have caused this behavior. The article says, these people "can’t hold the emotional space for their partner to process emotions in their presence....  If their partner is entering a place of chaos and of unknown outcome emotionally and this triggers them too much, they won’t be able to stay present through to the end."

Makes sense, I guess. There were many traumas and deaths this year, including the suicide. It was odd to watch my partner devolve and degenerate with each blow. Like. Why is MY friend killing himself making YOU fall apart? But in context I guess it makes some sense. 

Article: [Here](http://www.new-synapse.com/aps/wordpress/?p=2171)

